<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=communications-satellite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communications satellite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Comsat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A communications satellite is an artificial satellite that relays and amplifies radio signals through a transponder, carrying them around the curve of the Earth. In October 1945 Arthur C. Clarke described geostationary relays in a piece titled Extraterrestrial Relays. The first satellite built to relay communications actively was Project SCORE, launched on 18 December 1958, which stored a voice message on a tape recorder and sent a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> greeting from President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> before its batteries failed on 30 December. Telstar, launched on 10 July 1962, carried the first transatlantic television signals, and Syncom 3, on 19 July 1964, was the first geostationary one.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/communications-satellite.jpg" alt="Communications satellite" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr user ideonexus · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iridium_satellite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>18 December 1958</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>From 1951 the United States Naval Research Laboratory worked on Communication Moon Relay, which achieved transoceanic communication between Washington, D.C., and Hawaii on 23 January 1956 using the Moon as a passive relay; that used a natural body, not an artificial satellite.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/radio">Radio<span>1895</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-tape">Magnetic tape<span>1928</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=communications-satellite">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
